# TMS Integration Rules

## Purpose

These rules define how the automation framework interacts with Test Management Systems.

Current supported TMS integration target:

- Qase through MCP.

The goal is to use TMS cases as planning input, traceability source, and review context without blindly converting every TMS case into an automated test.

---

## Core Principle

TMS cases describe test intent and traceability.

Feature plans in `specs/<feature>/<feature>.md` decide how that intent should be automated.

Automated tests implement only the selected ready coverage.

Do not assume:

- one TMS case equals one Playwright test;
- every manual TMS step must become UI automation;
- every TMS case should be automated;
- TMS structure should override project architecture.

Use TMS for traceability and planning context.

Use feature plans to decide automation ownership.

Use API for backend contract and data predicates.

Use UI for distinct user-facing browser behavior.

Use schema validation for response shape.

Use TMS alignment to compare intent against planned or existing automation.

Do not let TMS structure force poor automation design.

---

## Recommended Workflow

When TMS cases exist, prefer this workflow:

1. Create or update the normal feature coverage plan in `specs/<feature>/<feature>.md`.
2. Align that feature plan with TMS cases.
3. Review the aligned feature plan.
4. Implement selected API coverage marked ready to implement now.
5. Implement selected UI coverage marked ready to implement now.
6. Review generated code.

Standard workflow: create or update the normal feature plan, then align that feature plan with TMS cases, then review, then implement selected ready coverage.

Future commands may automate the alignment step, but this rule must not depend on a command that is not yet created.

Do not implement directly from TMS cases unless explicitly requested.

---

## Default TMS Access Mode

Use TMS in read-only mode by default.

Allowed by default:

- list projects;
- list suites;
- read test cases;
- search or filter cases;
- read case metadata;
- use cases as planning input.

Not allowed unless explicitly requested:

- create test cases;
- update test cases;
- delete test cases;
- reorder suites or cases;
- create test runs;
- update test runs;
- publish test results;
- create defects;
- update defects;
- modify milestones, environments, custom fields, shared steps, or attachments.

If a prompt does not explicitly request TMS writes, use read-only mode.

---

## Qase MCP Usage

Use Qase MCP as the preferred mechanism for reading Qase data.

Required context for Qase tasks:

- provider: Qase;
- project code;
- suite id or suite path/name;
- case ids or filter, when needed;
- access mode.

If project code is missing and multiple projects exist, do not guess.

List projects and ask the user to choose, or report the blocker.

If suite is missing, list suites for the selected project and ask the user to choose, or report the blocker.

If Qase MCP is unavailable, report that MCP is unavailable and list available MCP servers.

When the task explicitly says Qase MCP only, do not use direct Qase API fallback.

When access mode is read-only MCP only, record that in the feature plan TMS Source section instead of using fallback API access.

---

## TMS Source In Feature Plans

When a feature plan uses TMS cases, include a TMS Source section in `specs/<feature>/<feature>.md`.

The feature plan should document:

- provider;
- project code;
- suite id;
- suite title or path;
- case ids reviewed;
- QQL/filter if used;
- access mode;
- source type when relevant, such as UI/manual or risk-based;
- whether MCP or fallback API was used, when applicable.

Example fields:

- provider:
- project code:
- suite id:
- suite title:
- cases reviewed:
- filter:
- access mode:
- source type:

---

## TMS Mapping In Feature Plans

When using TMS as planning input, add TMS Mapping to `specs/<feature>/<feature>.md`.

For each reviewed TMS case, capture:

- case id;
- title;
- source risk or intent;
- automation decision;
- target level;
- reason;
- planned automated scenario, if any;
- status.

Allowed automation decisions:

- API;
- UI;
- schema/contract;
- visual checkpoint;
- not automated;
- blocked;
- postponed.

Do not treat implementation details as TMS mapping decisions.

Helpers, builders, fixtures, clients, schemas, Page Objects, Component Objects, and assertion helpers are implementation decisions, not TMS cases.

---

## TMS Cases Written As UI Cases

TMS cases may be written as UI/manual scenarios.

Do not blindly preserve the TMS case level as the automation level.

A UI/manual TMS case may represent:

- backend contract risk;
- API predicate risk;
- UI interaction risk;
- visible feedback risk;
- schema/contract risk;
- data setup risk;
- visual risk.

Choose automation level by risk and available source of truth.

UI/manual TMS cases may map to API, schema/contract, UI, visual checkpoint, not automated, blocked, or postponed depending on risk and available source of truth.

If a TMS case describes backend behavior but no API contract/source is available, mark API coverage as blocked or postponed instead of inventing API assertions.

---

## Automation Decision Rules

Prefer API or schema/contract coverage when the TMS case describes:

- backend validation;
- request/response status;
- response body shape;
- authentication token behavior;
- authorization behavior;
- filtering predicate;
- sorting predicate;
- data transformation;
- persistence behavior;
- boundary behavior;
- documented negative behavior.

Prefer UI coverage when the TMS case describes:

- visible user journey;
- browser interaction;
- visible validation feedback;
- visible success or error state;
- navigation;
- frontend/backend integration visible to the user.

Prefer visual checkpoint only when the TMS case describes meaningful visual layout risk.

Use not automated when:

- automation cost is higher than value;
- behavior is better verified manually;
- observation is not reliable;
- requirement is unclear;
- product behavior is unstable.

Use blocked or postponed when:

- contract details are missing;
- status/body expectations are unclear;
- setup mechanism is missing;
- auth/role requirements are unclear;
- test data cannot be created safely;
- environment is unstable.

---

## Negative And Boundary Cases

TMS cases may include negative and boundary behavior.

Implement negative or boundary automation only when the contract or accepted source of truth defines expected behavior.

Do not guess:

- status codes;
- response bodies;
- validation messages;
- boundary limits;
- unsupported enum behavior;
- malformed request behavior.

If the TMS case describes a useful negative or boundary idea but the contract is unclear, mark it as blocked or postponed with reason.

---

## Alignment With Existing Feature Plans

When a feature plan already exists in `specs/<feature>/<feature>.md`, do not replace it blindly with TMS cases.

Instead:

- read the existing feature plan;
- read relevant TMS cases;
- compare TMS intent with planned coverage;
- add TMS Source and TMS Mapping;
- identify already covered cases;
- identify missing ready coverage;
- identify blocked or postponed cases;
- identify not automated cases;
- avoid duplicating existing coverage.

The result should be an aligned feature plan, not a direct copy of TMS cases.

---

## Existing Automation Comparison

When tests already exist, TMS alignment should identify:

- TMS cases already covered fully;
- TMS cases covered conceptually but not with exact TMS values;
- TMS cases partially covered;
- TMS cases not covered;
- TMS cases blocked by missing contract/setup;
- TMS cases intentionally not automated.

Do not add duplicate tests only to match exact TMS steps unless traceability requires exact values or behavior.

Prefer mapping existing automation to TMS intent when behavior is equivalent.

---

## Linking Automated Tests To TMS

TMS IDs may be added to specs or tests only after TMS mapping has been reviewed in `specs/<feature>/<feature>.md`.

Adding TMS IDs should follow project-approved metadata and reporting conventions.

Do not add reporter dependencies or result publishing without explicit approval.

When adding TMS IDs:

- keep scenario intent readable;
- avoid embedding TMS details in test titles unless project convention requires it;
- prefer project-approved metadata/reporting helpers when available.

Qase reporter integration and result publishing are separate from Qase MCP planning and TMS alignment.

Do not introduce reporter integration during planning or alignment unless explicitly requested.

---

## TMS Writes

TMS write operations require explicit user approval.

Before creating or updating TMS entities, the agent must report:

- target project;
- target suite;
- entities to create/update;
- exact fields to change;
- reason for change;
- dry-run summary.

Do not perform TMS writes during planning unless the user explicitly asks for TMS mutation.

---

## Security

Never commit real TMS tokens.

Do not store real Qase API tokens in repository files.

Do not print real tokens in reports.

Do not place real tokens in:

- specs;
- rules;
- skills;
- commands;
- project map;
- docs;
- test files;
- committed MCP config.

Use local Cursor MCP settings, environment variables, or secret storage for real tokens.

If a token is exposed in chat, logs, or git history, rotate it.

---

## Project Map

Project Map should document stable TMS conventions only.

Document:

- supported TMS provider;
- stable project code if appropriate;
- MCP server name;
- read-only default;
- where TMS mapping appears in feature plans;
- whether reporter integration is configured.

Do not document experimental TMS structure as stable convention.
